.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt BOOTBRIDGECTL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootbridgectl
.Nd inspect and steer a running bootbridge
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar flags
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
talks to the control socket of a
.Xr bootbridge 8
that is running, on the same machine: it reports the interfaces it
bridges and the peers it knows, prints statistics and the frames that
pass through, and changes what
.Xr bootbridge 8
prints while it runs.
Every command prints one line of JSON the bridge sends;
.Fl json
passes those lines through unchanged, for
.Xr jq 1 .
.Pp
The socket is
.Fl ctl
of
.Xr bootbridge 8 :
.Pa /run/bootbridge/bootbridge.sock
when it runs as root
.Pq or Pa /var/run/bootbridge/bootbridge.sock ,
where there is no Pa /run ,
a per-user socket under
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
or
.Pa /tmp
otherwise.
With several bridges on one machine, give
.Nm
.Fl socket
the path
.Xr bootbridge 8
logged when it started.
.Ev BOOTBRIDGE_CTL_SOCKET
is the default when no
.Fl socket
is given.
Without either,
.Nm
tries the system bridge's socket first, whoever runs it, so that members
of the socket's group can use it, then the user's own.
A socket that is missing or left behind by a stopped bridge is skipped;
one the user may not open is reported, with the group that may.
Anyone who can write the socket sees every frame bridged and can change
what is printed.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width "dump [-i what]"
.It Cm status
The bridge's version, uptime, configuration file, interface, MAC, socket
path, and packet printing settings.
.It Cm peers Oo Fl i Ar what Oc
The UDP peers of every transport: the configured ones, the ones learned
from the frames they sent, when each was last heard from, and which
Ethernet addresses were learned from each.
With the LAN side, the hosts seen there with their IP addresses, when each
last sent a frame, the protocols it used, and whether a RARP or BOOTP
request of the host is waiting for a reply.
.Fl i
limits the output to one side or to some transports; the default is
.Cm all .
.It Cm stats Oo Fl w Oc
Packets, bytes and errors per side, once, or every second with
.Fl w .
Then, per UDP transport, the datagrams it dropped: too short for a frame,
not an L2TPv3 data message, for another session, or with the wrong
cookie.
For L2TPv3 with sequence numbers, it also shows how many numbers were
skipped (frames lost, or still to arrive out of order), how many frames
arrived late (reordered or duplicated, and still delivered), and how often
a peer started over.
Raw transports show
.Li -
for the L2TPv3 counters.
.It Cm tail Oo Fl i Ar what Oc Oo Fl n Ar count Oc
The last
.Ar count
frames the bridge forwarded that
.Fl i
selects, 20 by default, 0 for all it remembers.
.It Cm tail Oo Fl t Ar count Oc Fl f
The remembered frames again, then every new one until interrupted;
.Fl t
says how many to replay (50 by default) and
.Fl n
stops after that many.
.It Cm log Oo Fl i Ar what Oc Oo Fl n Ar count Oc Oo Fl t Ar count Oc
Every frame as it passes, like
.Xr bootbridge 8
.Fl print .
.It Cm dump Oo Fl i Ar what Oc Oo Fl n Ar count Oc Oo Fl t Ar count Oc
The same with a hex and ASCII dump of each frame, like
.Xr bootbridge 8
.Fl X .
.It Cm find Oo Fl w Ar wait Oc Oo Fl qemu | Fl simh Oc
The bridges that announce themselves on the networks of this machine,
over DNS-SD and multicast DNS, found within
.Ar wait
(2s by default): their address, port, encapsulation, L2TPv3 session,
cookie length and sequence numbers, the interface they bridge, and the
local interface that heard them.
It does not use the control socket and works on any machine.
.Fl qemu
prints a QEMU
.Fl netdev
for each bridge instead, and
.Fl simh
a simh
.Ic attach xq
command for each raw one; the announcement leaves out the cookie, so
.Li COOKIE
stands for it, and the local port 4712 is any free port.
.It Cm set Oo Fl print Ar on Ns | Ns off Oc Oo Fl dump Ar on Ns | Ns off Oc Oo Fl iface Ar what Oc
Change what
.Xr bootbridge 8
prints to its own terminal, as its single keys do, and print the new
settings.
.El
.Pp
.Fl i
and
.Fl iface
take one of these, in any case:
.Bl -tag -width "transport" -offset indent
.It Cm auto
Every frame but LAN traffic that has nothing to do with the UDP side:
IPv6, spanning tree, LLDP, CDP and powerline management, anything to an
IPv4 multicast address (IGMP, mDNS, SSDP and the like), UDP broadcasts
to any port but BOOTP and DHCP (67, 68), TFTP (69), portmap (111) and PXE
(4011), which is where the discovery of Syncthing, Plex, Ubiquiti or
NetBIOS goes, ARP that is not about a host on the UDP side, and frames
that stay on the LAN.
Frames to a host on the UDP side are always shown, and so are the
broadcasts of other protocols, such as RARP, MOP, DECnet, AppleTalk and
IPX, so a boot request that nobody answers is seen too.
The default for
.Cm log ,
.Cm dump
and
.Cm tail .
.It Cm all
Every frame.
The default for
.Cm peers .
.It Cm lan
The LAN side, also by the interface's name: the frames from it, or with
.Cm peers ,
its hosts.
.It Cm udp
The UDP side, also by its name: the frames from it, or with
.Cm peers ,
its transports and hosts.
.It Ar transport
With
.Cm peers
only: a UDP transport by name or by any part of it that no other has,
such as its port, or
.Cm l2tp
or
.Cm raw
for the transports of that kind.
Frames are not told apart by transport.
.El
.Pp
A
.Ql -
or
.Ql \&!
in front selects everything else:
.Fl i Cm -lan
shows the frames from the UDP side.
Anything else is an error that lists what would work.
.Pp
All commands take
.Fl socket Ar path
.Pq but Cm find
and
.Fl json .
.Bl -tag -width "-socket path" -offset indent -compact
.It Fl socket Ar path
The control socket to talk to.
.It Fl json
Print the JSON lines the bridge sends.
.El
.Pp
A streaming command stops when the terminal interrupts it, when
.Xr bootbridge 8
stops, or when
.Fl n
frames have been printed.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 if the socket cannot be reached or the bridge
reports an error, and 2 for invalid usage.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask a bridge started as a service what it knows:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl status
.Dl # bootbridgectl peers
.Pp
Watch a client boot, remembering what passed before you started:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl dump -t 100
.Pp
Follow the frames of one side and count them:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl tail -f -i udp -n 20
.Pp
Everything, including the LAN noise
.Cm auto
leaves out:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl log -i all
.Pp
Turn on the bridge's own packet printing for the LAN side:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl set -print on -iface lan
.Pp
On another machine, find the bridges and connect QEMU to one:
.Pp
.Dl $ bootbridgectl find -qemu
.Pp
Script it:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl status -json | jq .uptime
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr bootbridge 8
